<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have achieved impressive performance across a range of mathematical tasks, from elementary arithmetic to high school olympiad-level problem solving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trinh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, to undergraduate-level challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsoukalas et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and even to open research problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glazer et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, despite this rapid progress, current benchmarks face several critical limitations—including low skill resolution, saturation at lower difficulty levels, and potential data contamination:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low skill resolution.</span> Most benchmarks report only top-level accuracy, without disentangling the underlying skills required. For instance, solving an algebra problem may rely on applying known theorems, symbolic computation, proof writing, and intuitive judgment—but current benchmarks do not identify which of these a model succeeded or failed on.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Saturation of existing benchmarks.</span> Datasets like GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and MATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite> focus primarily on elementary and high school mathematics. While these have served as important early-stage benchmarks, state-of-the-art models now routinely achieve near-perfect accuracy on them, limiting their utility in assessing more advanced mathematical thinking or research-level reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark contamination.</span> Many benchmark problems are scraped from online sources and may have leaked into training corpora, making performance on them less reliable as a measure of genuine reasoning ability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These limitations hinder meaningful diagnosis of a model’s true capabilities, making it very difficult, if not impossible, to attribute its successes or failures to certain capabilities or lack thereof. Hence the evaluations cannot be scientifically rigorous or conclusive regarding whether the model truly understands certain abstract concepts or has grasped certain mathematical skills. This impedes systematic development of new methods to improve or revise current practices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Several recent efforts have begun to push beyond the limitations above. In particular, <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">FrontierMath</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glazer et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces a benchmark with all-level problems and proof-based reasoning tasks, helping to address the saturation and contamination of existing benchmarks. However, it primarily evaluates task-level correctness across domains and does not provide structured annotations of the underlying cognitive skills or reasoning behaviors. As such, it does not directly resolve the challenges of low skill resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At a more fundamental level, general intelligence can be viewed as the capacity to extract low-dimensional structures from complex data and to exploit such structures for prediction, reasoning, decision making, and creative problem solving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Buchanan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. There are reasons to believe that mathematics requires a more advanced form of intelligence though, formally known as (part of) <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the</span> Artificial Intelligence, that is unique to educated and enlightened humans. It goes way beyond identifying and exploiting low-dimensional structures (or correlations) in data distributions. It is not only about solving certain problems correctly based on exploiting correlation with past practices or experiences, but also demonstrating grasp of a broad spectrum of capabilities—from understanding abstract concepts and applying the associated knowledge, to constructing and deducing proofs, leveraging intuition, transferring across domains, and even posing novel problems and conjectures. Traditional benchmarks, by focusing narrowly on correctness, provide only a coarse snapshot of performance and thus fail to capture this multifaceted nature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we introduce GAUSS (<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>eneral <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>ssessment of <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">U</span>nderlying <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>tructured <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S</span>kills in Mathematics), a benchmark that evaluates mathematical proficiency through a fine-grained lens of mathematical skills and behaviors—rather than coarse topical labels or problem types.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">First, to address the problem of <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">low skill resolution</em>, GAUSS organizes tasks by the specific mathematical abilities they require, not merely by topic. Each problem is annotated with cognitive skill tags—such as theorem recall, symbolic manipulation, strategic problem-solving, or intuitive judgment—enabling detailed diagnostics of a model’s strengths and weaknesses. This structure reveals whether performance arises from superficial pattern matching or genuine understanding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Second, to overcome the <em id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">saturation of existing benchmarks</em>, GAUSS targets high-level tasks beyond elementary and high school curricula. Most of its problems are drawn from advanced undergraduate and graduate topics, as well as elite mathematical competitions (e.g., the IMO, national Olympiads, and collegiate contests). These challenging problems push the evaluation frontier toward higher-order reasoning and research-level mathematical thinking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Third, to mitigate <em id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">benchmark contamination</em>, GAUSS draws on a broad range of sources unlikely to have appeared in model training data. These include lesser-known competition problems, curated textbook materials, online forum problems, and selected research-level tasks, among others. By integrating such diverse sources while minimizing overlap with widely available public corpora, GAUSS enables a more faithful assessment of genuine reasoning ability rather than surface-level memorization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, GAUSS shifts the focus from topic-based correctness to skill-based competence, providing a more interpretable, robust, and forward-looking benchmark for evaluating mathematical abilities in large language models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We organize mathematical proficiency into three overarching domains encompassing twelve core cognitive skills:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mathematical Knowledge and Understanding.</span>
This domain includes knowledge recall, conceptual understanding, and application of known results.</p>
<ul id="S1.I2.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memorizing mathematical knowledge:</span> Recall of definitions, theorems, lemmas, and subject taxonomy across algebra, analysis, combinatorics, etc.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Understanding of knowledge and theories:</span> Know what classical theorems state, why they are true, and how to apply them effectively.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational and analytical skills:</span> Perform accurate symbolic computations and algebraic transformations.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Solving and Communication.</span>
This domain focuses on tackling problems through reasoning and effectively conveying mathematical ideas.</p>
<ul id="S1.I2.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem-solving framework:</span> Understand problems, explore examples, and apply key strategies like contradiction, decomposition, and analogy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logical thinking and reasoning:</span> Construct valid proofs and critically evaluate existing arguments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Writing and presentation:</span> Express solutions clearly and tailor explanations for different audiences.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning, Meta Skills, and Creativity.</span>
This domain reflects the learner’s ability to acquire new knowledge independently, adapt across contexts, and engage in abstract, cross-disciplinary, and creative reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I2.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning new knowledge:</span> Read and absorb advanced content, and independently acquire unfamiliar techniques.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intuition:</span> Use geometric and numerical insights, plausibility checks, and aesthetic judgment to guide and validate solutions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Meta skills:</span> Think abstractly and transfer tools across domains (e.g., probabilistic method in combinatorics).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mathematical modeling:</span> Translate real-world or theoretical scenarios into formal mathematical models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization:</span> Abstract patterns and unify results across different contexts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Creativity:</span> Pose new problems, invent novel methods, and apply lateral thinking to discover unconventional solutions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">GAUSS is designed to be <span id="S1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">diagnostic</span>, <span id="S1.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">extensible</span>, and <span id="S1.p12.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">robust to contamination</span>. By evaluating models through a skill-oriented lens, GAUSS complements existing benchmarks and provides a new foundation for tracking genuine mathematical progress in LLMs—especially as they move beyond rote learning and toward autonomous discovery and research.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the value of the GAUSS benchmark, we apply it to GPT-5-thinking and yield a fine-grained profile of the model’s mathematical skills. The model shows <em id="S1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">notable strengths</em> in <span id="S1.p13.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">recalling the taxonomy of mathematical subjects</span>, in <span id="S1.p13.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">evaluating arguments</span>, in <span id="S1.p13.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">posing novel problems</span>, in <span id="S1.p13.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">writing proofs</span>, in <span id="S1.p13.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reading advanced materials</span> and in <span id="S1.p13.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">demonstrating strong intuition</span> through abilities such as plausibility checks. These abilities reflect reliable performance in knowledge organization, error detection, intuitive reasoning, problem formulation, and comprehension of technical content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the model’s performance is <em id="S1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">limited</em> in core areas such as <span id="S1.p14.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">theorem understanding</span>, <span id="S1.p14.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">computational and analytical skills</span> and <span id="S1.p14.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">problem-solving strategies</span>, with further weaknesses in <span id="S1.p14.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">generalization</span>. These results suggest persistent difficulties in applying known results, carrying out symbolic computations, applying systematic strategies and developing unifying frameworks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Relative to o4-mini-high, GPT-5-thinking achieves measurable improvements in <span id="S1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">subject taxonomy</span>, <span id="S1.p15.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">argument evaluation</span>, <span id="S1.p15.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">plausibility check</span>, and <span id="S1.p15.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reading advanced materials</span>, while most other categories show only marginal differences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">At present, GAUSS remains limited in scope: the problem pool is still relatively small, subject coverage is incomplete, and open-ended tasks such as proofs and creative reasoning are difficult to grade. To address these limitations, we plan to broaden the benchmark through community-sourced problems across mathematical domains, design automatic grading pipelines, and develop an open, extensible platform for integrating new problems and evaluation modules. In this way, we aim to construct more precise skill profiles, revealing where models truly excel and where they continue to struggle.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Math Skills Breakdown</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1 </span>Memory of Math Knowledge</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This category focuses on the ability to recall essential mathematical knowledge, including definitions, theorems, and lemmas. It forms the foundation of all higher-level reasoning and problem-solving. Two key components define this category: a subject-based taxonomy of mathematical domains and key statements within each domain.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">(a) </span>Taxonomy of subjects</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mathematical knowledge is traditionally organized into subjects such as:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Algebra</span>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Analysis</span>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Combinatorics</span>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Geometry and Topology</span>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Probability and Statistics</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This subject taxonomy provides a conceptual “map” for indexing, retrieving, and organizing mathematical knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Design Principles:</span> Choose concepts that are familiar to domain experts but are not typically found in standard textbooks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample Problems:</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.SS1.SSS1.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.fig1.1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:276.0pt;">
            
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="225.85" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 381.9 225.85" width="381.9"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,225.85) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3D6666;" fill="#3D6666" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 221.22 C 0 223.78 2.07 225.85 4.63 225.85 L 377.27 225.85 C 379.83 225.85 381.9 223.78 381.9 221.22 L 381.9 4.63 C 381.9 2.07 379.83 0 377.27 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 206.75 L 381.21 206.75 L 381.21 4.63 C 381.21 2.45 379.45 0.69 377.27 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 213.54)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:25.16em;--ltx-fo-height:0.56em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="348.14"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:25.16em;">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Problem 0: Floer homology</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:29.01em;--ltx-fo-height:13.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="190.32" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 188.16)" width="401.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:29.01em;">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Floer homology is a powerful tool for studying symplectic geometry and low-dimensional topology.</span></span>
<span id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<span id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<span id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please name three distinct Floer homology theories that play important roles in low-dimensional topology.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<span id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For each theory, provide an example of a significant topological application. Your applications should not mention explicitly the Floer homology theory in the statement.</span></span>
</span></span>
</span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" color="#FF0000" height="18.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.1px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 18.24 18.24" width="18.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FF0000;--ltx-fill-color:#FF0000;" fill="#FF0000" stroke="#FF0000" stroke-width="1.8pt" transform="translate(0,18.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.25,0) translate(0,1.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 15.75 15.75 M 0 15.75 L 15.75 0"></path></g></svg></span>
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">o4-mini-high failed<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> <span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.pic2" class="ltx_picture" color="#008000" height="18.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.1px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 18.24 18.24" width="18.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#009900;--ltx-fill-color:#009900;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" color="#009900" fill="#009900" stroke="#009900" stroke-width="1.8pt" transform="translate(0,18.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.25,0) translate(0,1.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 5.51 0 L 15.75 15.75"></path></g></svg></span><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p4.pic1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">
GPT-5 thinking passed</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
          
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.fig1.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:276.0pt;">
            
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="169.33" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 381.9 169.33" width="381.9"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,169.33) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3D6666;" fill="#3D6666" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 164.7 C 0 167.26 2.07 169.33 4.63 169.33 L 377.27 169.33 C 379.83 169.33 381.9 167.26 381.9 164.7 L 381.9 4.63 C 381.9 2.07 379.83 0 377.27 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 150.23 L 381.21 150.23 L 381.21 4.63 C 381.21 2.45 379.45 0.69 377.27 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 157.02)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:25.16em;--ltx-fo-height:0.56em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.69)" width="348.14"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:25.16em;">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Problem 0: Liouville Quantum Gravity</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:29.01em;--ltx-fo-height:9.51em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="133.8" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 131.64)" width="401.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:29.01em;">
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Liouville quantum gravity (LQG) is a central topic in modern probability theory, please list at least 3 major subfields, and for each:</span></span>
<span id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<span id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<span id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Name a central concept or structure,</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<span id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">State one foundational result from that area,</span></span>
</span></span>
</span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" color="#008000" height="18.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.1px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 18.24 18.24" width="18.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#009900;--ltx-fill-color:#009900;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" color="#009900" fill="#009900" stroke="#009900" stroke-width="1.8pt" transform="translate(0,18.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.25,0) translate(0,1.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 5.51 0 L 15.75 15.75"></path></g></svg></span>
<span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p5.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">GPT-5 thinking passed</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
          
</div></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">(b) </span>Key mathematical statements</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within each domain, learners should be able to recall and accurately state:</p>
<ol id="S2.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Definitions</span> — E.g., Define a compact set in a metric space; define a <math id="S2.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theorems</span> — E.g., State the Intermediate Value Theorem; state Fermat’s Little Theorem.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Frequently Used Lemmas and Tricks</span> — E.g., What is the pigeonhole principle? State Chebyshev’s inequality.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These key mathematical statements form the building blocks of further reasoning and problem-solving.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Design Principles</span>:
Choose mathematical statements that are familiar to domain experts but are not typically found in standard textbooks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample Problems:</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.SS1.SSS2.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.fig1.1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:276.0pt;">
            
