<td id="S3.T3.5.31.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.5.31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mathematical Modeling</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.32" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.32.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.32.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.32.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Translating real-world problems</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.32.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.32.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.32.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T3.5.32.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">0</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.32.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.32.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.32.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Difficulty formalizing assumptions/variables.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.33" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">
<td id="S3.T3.5.33.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.5.33.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.34" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.34.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.34.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.34.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Identifying patterns</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.34.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.34.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.34.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T3.5.34.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">0</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.34.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.34.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.34.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Patterns not reliably detected from examples.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.35" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.35.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.35.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.35.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Developing unifying theories</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.35.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFECC8;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.35.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.35.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T3.5.35.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFECC8;">33</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.35.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.35.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.35.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Occasional synthesis, but lacks consistency.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.36" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">
<td id="S3.T3.5.36.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T3.5.36.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Creativity</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.37" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.37.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.37.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.37.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Posing new problems</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.37.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="--ltx-bg-color:#C8FFC8;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.37.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.37.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T3.5.37.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#C8FFC8;">100</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.37.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.37.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.37.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Generates meaningful, well-posed questions.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.38" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.38.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.38.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.38.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Inventing methods</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.38.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="--ltx-bg-color:#C8FFC8;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.38.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.38.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T3.5.38.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#C8FFC8;">100</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.38.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T3.5.38.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.38.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposes original methods.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.5.39" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.5.39.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S3.T3.5.39.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:113.8pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.39.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Lateral thinking</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.39.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.39.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:56.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.39.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T3.5.39.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFC8C8;">0</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T3.5.39.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="S3.T3.5.39.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T3.5.39.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Rarely makes unconventional connections.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall Assessment:</span>
GPT-5-thinking exhibits clear strengths across both memory- and reasoning-oriented abilities. Notably, it achieves perfect performance in recalling the <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">taxonomy of mathematical subjects</span> (memory), in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">evaluating existing arguments</span> (reasoning), in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">plausibility checks</span> (intuition), in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reading advanced materials</span> (learning), and in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p8.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">problem posing</span> (creativity). These outcomes highlight competence in knowledge organization, error detection, intuitive judgment, comprehension of technical material, and problem formulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, GPT-5-thinking performs poorly in foundational areas including <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">understanding of knowledge and theories</span> (0%), <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">computational and analytical skills</span> (0%), <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">application of
problem-solving strategies</span> (0%), and <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mathematical modeling</span> (0%), with additional weakness in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p9.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">generalization</span> (17%). These deficiencies indicate persistent difficulties in understanding/applying theorems, executing symbolic computations, applying systematic strategies in problem solving, and developing unifying frameworks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, GPT-5-thinking demonstrates strong capabilities in structuring mathematical domains, absorbing advanced material, spotting logical flaws, leveraging intuition, and posing problems. However, its higher-order reasoning remains fragile without stronger mastery of core computational and theoretical skills.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison with o4-mini-high:</span>
Relative to o4-mini-high, GPT-5-thinking shows measurable improvements in the <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">taxonomy of mathematical subjects</span> (memory), in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p11.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">evaluating existing arguments</span> (reasoning), in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p11.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">plausibility check</span> (intuition), in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p11.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">writing proofs</span> (writing) and in <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p11.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reading advanced materials</span> (learning). In most other categories, however, performance gains are marginal and the overall skill profile remains largely unchanged. This comparison should be interpreted with caution, as a full evaluation of o4-mini-high is not available.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Future Directions</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We believe that the benchmark framework proposed in this paper establishes a principled basis for evaluating large language models (LLMs) in mathematical reasoning, shifting the focus from overall accuracy to a more interpretable, skill-based understanding. Rather than viewing LLMs as monolithic solvers, this decomposition allows us to assess which cognitive abilities—such as memory, symbolic fluency, logical structure, or creative strategy—are emerging and which remain weak or brittle. By grounding evaluation in human-like skill taxonomies, we aim to better understand not only what models can do, but how they reason and where that reasoning begins to break down.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Systematic skill profiling across models</span> will be crucial for charting progress. Current LLMs display uneven capabilities—often excelling in algebraic manipulation and syntactic logic, while faltering on tasks requiring abstraction, creativity, or generalization. Such skill profiles can feed into radar-style visualizations, where each axis corresponds to a distinct mathematical competency, as the example shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4 Future Directions ‣ GAUSS: Benchmarking Structured Mathematical Skills for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. These visual summaries not only help interpret models’ strengths and weaknesses but also serve as diagnostic tools for deployment contexts. Coupled with longitudinal tracking of models over time, we can better understand the trajectory of skill emergence, detect stagnation, and identify developmental bottlenecks that resist improvement despite scaling. We plan to test this framework by generating skill profiles for a variety of models, including both open-source and proprietary LLMs. For each mathematical skill, we will evaluate models on representative tasks and compare their performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we can compare how performance varies across different training and fine-tuning strategies. This enables not only cross-sectional comparisons across model families, but also longitudinal studies that track how specific abilities develop—such as whether generalization emerges earlier in instruction-tuned models, or whether abstraction improves significantly only after reinforcement learning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.18122v1/sample_radar_chart.jpg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/381;" width="476" height="381" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Example LLM Math Skills Radar Chart</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Auto-evaluation infrastructure</span> also deserves careful attention. While some benchmark modules—such as computational fluency, symbolic computation, and logic verification—can be reliably auto-graded, other modules require more nuanced assessment strategies. Tasks that involve constructing proofs, generating multiple solution paths, or synthesizing definitions from context may require hybrid pipelines that integrate rubric-based scoring, LLM-assisted grading heuristics, and human-in-the-loop review. Building such pipelines in a modular and interpretable way is key to scaling evaluation while maintaining reliability. Additionally, publishing these pipelines can promote reproducibility and encourage community benchmarking beyond academic labs and foundation model developers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Some subjective problems are inherently difficult to grade—especially those involving mathematical exposition, abstraction, or creative reasoning. To address this, we will experiment with prompt-chained LLM grading, where one model proposes a solution and another provides a structured critique based on rubric-aligned criteria. These auto-graders will be calibrated against human-graded gold standards to measure alignment and detect blind spots. In addition, for high-stakes or ambiguous tasks, we will design interfaces for expert-in-the-loop review, allowing humans to overrule, annotate, or co-grade with the model. By integrating both automatic and human judgment in a layered fashion, we aim to balance scalability with rigor and ensure that evaluation remains faithful to the underlying reasoning quality, not just surface correctness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Crowd-sourcing problems</span> can further enrich the benchmark by diversifying both content and skill coverage. Through contributions from mathematically skilled volunteers, we can source novel, high-quality problems spanning a wide range of difficulty levels and contexts. To preserve diagnostic value, each contributed problem will be annotated with both its <em id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">skill category</em> (e.g., symbolic manipulation, logical reasoning, creative problem solving) and its <em id="S4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">subject taxonomy</em>—a hierarchical classification of the mathematical domain (e.g., algebra, geometry, combinatorics). This dual tagging enables fine-grained analysis across the skill–subject matrix and facilitates the identification of underrepresented areas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Building an extensible platform</span> will enable researchers to easily plug in new tasks, rubrics, and grading modules without modifying the core infrastructure. By open-sourcing the framework and encouraging community contributions, we aim to create a flexible, modular ecosystem that supports ongoing innovation in benchmark design and evaluation methodology. This will help establish shared standards for assessing mathematical capabilities in LLMs that are acceptable by both the mathematics and technology communities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Ultimately, our goal is to bridge the gap between memory-level fluency and genuine mathematical understanding and insight. We hope that by embedding this skill-centric perspective into model training, evaluation, and deployment, we can push toward systems that do not merely imitate solutions, but engage in structured, interpretable, and flexible reasoning that reflects a deeper grasp of mathematics. This shift, from solving problems to understanding them, marks a necessary evolution in the role of LLMs in mathematical reasoning and scientific discovery. Viewed in this light, GAUSS represents not only a benchmark, but also a cornerstone in the roadmap toward achieving general artificial intelligence in mathematics and science.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
