</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4 </span>Problem-Solving Framework</h3>

<section id="A2.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">(a) </span>Problem Interpretation and Analysis</h4>

<section id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Problem 4a1</h5>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:-10.0pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="365.37" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 365.37" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,365.37) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3D6666;" fill="#3D6666" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 360.74 C 0 363.3 2.07 365.37 4.63 365.37 L 425.01 365.37 C 427.56 365.37 429.64 363.3 429.64 360.74 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 346.5 L 428.94 346.5 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 351.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:24.44em;">
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Problem</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.01em;--ltx-fo-height:23.13em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="322.19" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 320.04)" width="442.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.01em;">
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A, B, C, D, E, and F are sitting in a circle, each wearing a hat. Each hat is either pure black, pure white, or has an integer written on it. Everyone knows that there is at least one black hat, one white hat, and one numbered hat, and that the numbers on the numbered hats are exactly a sequence of consecutive integers (which can be just one number).</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Each person can see everyone else’s hat, but not their own. The host asks if they know what hat they are wearing (they must know the exact color or the exact number, unless they know they are the only one with a numbered hat—in which case, even without knowing the number’s value, it is considered “knowing” their hat). They answer in turn as follows (everyone is perfectly logical and honest):</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A: I know.
B: I don’t know.
C: I know.
D: I know.
E: I don’t know.
F: I know.</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Seeing E’s puzzled expression, D adds:
“I didn’t know what hat I was wearing at first, but after hearing what C said, I knew.”</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">E suddenly understands.</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Question: Who is wearing colored hats, and who is wearing numbered hats?</span></span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" color="#FF0000" height="18.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.1px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 18.24 18.24" width="18.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FF0000;--ltx-fill-color:#FF0000;" fill="#FF0000" stroke="#FF0000" stroke-width="1.8pt" transform="translate(0,18.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.25,0) translate(0,1.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 15.75 15.75 M 0 15.75 L 15.75 0"></path></g></svg></span>
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">GPT-5 thinking failed</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proposed by Qiuyu Ren</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Response of GPT-5-thinking</span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">No single arrangement is forced by the conversation. Two (and only two) patterns fit all statements, including D’s “I only knew after C spoke” and E’s later realization:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">1. Numbers on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B,C,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B,C,D</annotation></semantics></math>; colors on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,E,F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,E,F</annotation></semantics></math>
2. Numbers on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,E,F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,E,F</annotation></semantics></math>; colors on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B,C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B,C</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Reasoning sketch:
- <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> says “I know” immediately <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> among the five hats <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> sees, all colored hats are the same color, so <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the missing color. Thus <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is colored.
- <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> then says “I know”. This can happen in two ways:
(i) <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is numbered, because <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> have numbers forming a nearly-complete consecutive sequence, leaving only one possible value for <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> numbers on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B,C,D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B,C,D</annotation></semantics></math> (case 1);
(ii) <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is colored: from <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>’s immediate certainty, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> infers his own color from the pattern he sees <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> numbers on <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,E,F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,E,F</annotation></semantics></math> (case 2).
- <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>’s remark “I did not know at first; I knew after C” is compatible with both layouts: in case 1, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>’s number tells <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> his place in the run; in case 2, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>’s color confirms that <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is numbered.
- <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is initially unsure but learns after D’s remark (in case 1, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> learns he is colored; in case 2, he learns his exact number).
- <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> knows at the end in both layouts.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Example configurations:
Case 1: <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\text{black}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>black</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\text{black}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\text{white}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>white</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\text{white}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\text{white}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>white</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\text{white}</annotation></semantics></math>
Case 2: <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\text{black}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>black</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\text{black}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\text{white}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>white</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\text{white}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\text{white}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>white</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\text{white}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=2</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard Solution</span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">B,C,D are wearing colored hats and A,E,F are wearing numbered hats.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Some observations:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">1. A person knows their hat without any information from conversations if they are wearing the unique black, white, or numbered hat, or if they are wearing a numbered hat whose number is not extremal.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">2. If there are exactly two numbered hats, under no circumstance do the people wearing numbered hats know their hats.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">3. The last person among <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\sim F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sim F</annotation></semantics></math> who is wearing a black (resp. white) hat knows their hat after hearing the earlier conversations among people wearing the same hat as theirs.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is because either all people wearing the same hat ahead answer “don’t know” from which he can infer his hat, or someone <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> among them answers “know,” hence he must know for the same reason as <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">4. C,D,E are wearing mutually different hats.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly E is a wearing different hat from CD’s. If D is wearing the same hat as C, then they don’t need to rely on C’s answer, a contradiction.
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Back to the problem. Without loss of generality, say the minimal number on numbered hats is <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the number of black hats is no less than the number of white ones. We divide into 4 cases according to the number of numbered hats.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p16.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: There is one numbered hat.
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Case 1.1<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p16.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: The hats are <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bbbbw1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bbbbw1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">By observation 1, both D and E are wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts observation 4.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1.2<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p18.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: The hats are <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bbbww1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bbbww1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">A must wear <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts observation 4.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p20.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: There are two numbered hats.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">The fact that everyone except possibly B eventually knows their hat contradicts observation 2.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p22.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: There are three numbered hats.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus the hats are <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bbw123" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>123</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bbw123</annotation></semantics></math>. A is not wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b,1,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b,1,3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3.1<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p24.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: A is wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">By observation 2, the people wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,3</annotation></semantics></math> know their hat. Now by observation 1,3, at most one person (wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>) does not know their hat, contradiction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3.2<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p26.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: A is wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then everyone else knows A wears the unique <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> after A’s answer. B is wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. Without loss of generality B is not wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3.2.1<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p28.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: B is wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then by observation 1,3, D,E are not wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence one of them is wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the other wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. By symmetry, E should have answered “know,” contradiction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3.2.2<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p30.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: B is wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">One can know deduce in order that <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\sim F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sim F</annotation></semantics></math> are wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w123bb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>123</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w123bb</annotation></semantics></math>. The answers are consistent (an alternative for E before D’s clarification is <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w1234b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1234</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w1234b</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 4<span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p32.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: There are four numbered hats.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">The hats are <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="bw1234" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1234</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">bw1234</annotation></semantics></math>. By observation 1, B,E are wearing <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,4</annotation></semantics></math>, and consequently D should have know their hat before C’s answer, contradiction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">In conclusion, the only possibility of hat arrangements (up to symmetries) is <math id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w123bb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>123</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w123bb</annotation></semantics></math> from Case 3.2.2.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS4.SSS1.Px1.p35.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="69.62" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 69.62" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,69.62) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#4D4D4D;" fill="#4D4D4D" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 65 C 0 67.55 2.07 69.62 4.63 69.62 L 425.01 69.62 C 427.56 69.62 429.64 67.55 429.64 65 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 50.76 L 428.94 50.76 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 55.39)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
