</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8 </span>Meta Skills</h3>

<section id="A2.SS8.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">(a) </span>Thinking abstractly</h4>

<section id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Problem 8a1 </h5>

<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:-10.0pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="114.15" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 114.15" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,114.15) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3D6666;" fill="#3D6666" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 109.52 C 0 112.08 2.07 114.15 4.63 114.15 L 425.01 114.15 C 427.56 114.15 429.64 112.08 429.64 109.52 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 95.29 L 428.94 95.29 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 99.92)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:24.44em;">
<span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Problem</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.01em;--ltx-fo-height:4.97em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="70.97" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 68.82)" width="442.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.01em;">
<span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Can a nonempty open interval be covered by countably many disjoint closed intervals?</span></span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" color="#FF0000" height="18.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.1px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 18.24 18.24" width="18.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FF0000;--ltx-fill-color:#FF0000;" fill="#FF0000" stroke="#FF0000" stroke-width="1.8pt" transform="translate(0,18.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.25,0) translate(0,1.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 15.75 15.75 M 0 15.75 L 15.75 0"></path></g></svg></span>
<span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">GPT-5 thinking failed</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proposed by Tahsin Saffat</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Response of GPT-5-thinking</span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Short answer:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">* If “cover” means just “contained in the union,” then **yes**—the single closed interval <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a,b]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a,b]</annotation></semantics></math> covers <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> (a one-element family is countable and vacuously disjoint).
* If you mean **write <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> as a disjoint union of closed intervals contained in it**, then **no**.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Why not? Suppose <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)=\bigsqcup_{n\geq 1}[c_{n},d_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)=\bigsqcup_{n\geq 1}[c_{n},d_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[c_{n},d_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[c_{n},d_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise disjoint and each <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[c_{n},d_{n}]\subset(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[c_{n},d_{n}]\subset(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. If there are at least two such intervals, pick two with <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}&lt;c_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}&lt;c_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the open gap <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{i},c_{j})\subset(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{i},c_{j})\subset(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> contains no points of the union—a contradiction. Hence there can be at most one interval. But a single closed interval cannot equal the open interval <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore it’s impossible.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard Solution</span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Source: <a href="https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2010/10/04/covering-a-non-closed-interval-by-disjoint-closed-intervals/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2010/10/04/covering-a-non-closed-interval-by-disjoint-closed-intervals/</a></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We show that <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a countable disjoint union of closed intervals. This is equivalent to the problem.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">As each interval is bounded, we need an infinite number of intervals <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}=[a_{n},b_{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}=[a_{n},b_{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> to cover the real line. Now consider the set</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A2.Ex222" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex222.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:=\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}\{a_{n},b_{n}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:=\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}\{a_{n},b_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">consisting of the endpoints of the intervals <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is countably infinite. Also, as the <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> form a disjoint cover of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement of the open set <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}(a_{n},b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty}(a_{n},b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and is hence closed. Finally, we claim that <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is perfect: that not only is <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> closed, but every point in <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a limit point in <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, if <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is either the left or right endpoint of an interval <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, but it is not both. If it is, say, the right-endpoint of an interval, then by approaching <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> from the right we see that <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the limit of the left endpoint of intervals to the right of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we appeal to a general theorem (a special case of the even more general Baire category theorem) that asserts that a perfect subset of a complete metric space cannot be countably infinite. A proof is as follows. Suppose for contradiction that <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\{x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a countably infinite perfect set. Let <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be any closed ball of positive radius whose centre lies in E (e.g. one can take the closed ball of radius <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> centered at <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>). Using the fact that <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a limit point, one can then find a closed ball <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> whose centre still lies in <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, but which is disjoint from <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and whose radius is at most half that of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Indeed, if <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> does not already lie in the interior of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, one simply has to shrink <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by a little bit and recenter to another element of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> to move <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> off the boundary of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, one uses the limit point property to find another point in the interior of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> other than <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and makes a small closed ball around that.) Then one can find a closed ball <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of radius at most half that of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> whose centre still lies in <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, but is now also disjoint from <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in addition to <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Continuing in this fashion we find a sequence <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1},B_{2},B_{3},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1},B_{2},B_{3},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> of nested closed balls with radii going to zero, each of which has centre in <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. The centres of these balls then form a Cauchy sequence, which by completeness, must converge to a limit in <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> that also lies in <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty}B_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty}B_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. But by construction, <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty}B_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋂</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty}B_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> lies outside each element <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, and is thus disjoint from <math id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p12.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS8.SSS1.Px1.p14.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="53.02" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 53.02" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,53.02) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#4D4D4D;" fill="#4D4D4D" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 48.39 C 0 50.95 2.07 53.02 4.63 53.02 L 425.01 53.02 C 427.56 53.02 429.64 50.95 429.64 48.39 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 34.15 L 428.94 34.15 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 38.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
