</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">Problem 9a2 </h5>

<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:-10.0pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="271.43" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 271.43" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,271.43) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3D6666;" fill="#3D6666" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 266.8 C 0 269.36 2.07 271.43 4.63 271.43 L 425.01 271.43 C 427.56 271.43 429.64 269.36 429.64 266.8 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 252.57 L 428.94 252.57 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 257.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:24.44em;">
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Problem</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.01em;--ltx-fo-height:16.34em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="228.26" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 226.1)" width="442.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.01em;">
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are reading the paper <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.16093" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href"> Multiple chordal SLE(<math class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>) and quantum Calogero-Moser system </a> and encounter the following concept: <span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Local multiple SLE systems</span>.</span></span>
<span id="A2.I26" class="ltx_enumerate">
<span id="A2.I26.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<span id="A2.I26.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I26.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I26.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Identify the concept and explain its context.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I26.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<span id="A2.I26.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I26.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I26.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Restate the assumptions formally.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I26.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<span id="A2.I26.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I26.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I26.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Why do we call it a local system?</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I26.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<span id="A2.I26.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I26.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I26.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How do we define the compatible family of measures?</span></span>
</span></span>
</span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" color="#008000" height="18.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.1px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 18.24 18.24" width="18.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#009900;--ltx-fill-color:#009900;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" color="#009900" fill="#009900" stroke="#009900" stroke-width="1.8pt" transform="translate(0,18.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.25,0) translate(0,1.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 5.51 0 L 15.75 15.75"></path></g></svg></span>
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">GPT-5 thinking passed</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proposed by Jiaxin Zhang</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Response of GPT-5-thinking</span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Set-up.<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Let <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be simply connected; let <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},\dots,z_{n}\in\partial\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},\dots,z_{n}\in\partial\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct boundary prime ends (“seed” points), and let <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\partial\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\partial\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> be an additional marked boundary point (used for normalization, e.g. <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>).
For each <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, fix a closed neighborhood <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}\subset\overline{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}\subset\overline{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}\cap U_{j}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}\cap U_{j}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\notin U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\notin U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1) Concept &amp; context.<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
A <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local multiple chordal SLE<math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> system</em> assigns, to every choice of disjoint neighborhoods <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},\dots,U_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},\dots,U_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as above, a law on the <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">initial curve pieces</em> that start at <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and are grown only until they exit <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This assignment is required to be conformally covariant, to satisfy a local domain Markov/commutation property, and to be absolutely continuous with respect to independent chordal SLEs via a smooth positive <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">partition function</em>.
It provides a way to specify the joint law of several interfaces <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">near their seeds</em> without a global specification of full curves; it is complementary to the “global” construction (a single measure on full <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of disjoint curves).</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2) Formal assumptions (axioms).<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
A <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local multiple chordal SLE<math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math></em> on <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u)</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of probability measures</span></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex272" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex272.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">on <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of simple curves <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta^{(1)},\dots,\eta^{(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta^{(1)},\dots,\eta^{(n)})</annotation></semantics></math> such that each <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> starts at <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, is contained in <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> up to its exit time from <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I27" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I27.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I27.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I27.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I27.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Conformal covariance.</em> For any conformal map <math id="A2.I27.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\Omega\to\Omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\Omega\to\Omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which respects the chosen normalization (e.g. <math id="A2.I27.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(u)=u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(u)=u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>), the push-forward of <math id="A2.I27.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex273" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex273.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(\phi(U_{1}),\dots,\phi(U_{n}))}_{\Omega^{\prime};\phi(z_{1}),\dots,\phi(z_{n});\phi(u)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(\phi(U_{1}),\dots,\phi(U_{n}))}_{\Omega^{\prime};\phi(z_{1}),\dots,\phi(z_{n});\phi(u)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I27.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I27.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I27.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I27.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Local domain Markov / commutation.</em> If one reveals initial segments of the curves up to stopping times <math id="A2.I27.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> that are bounded by the exit times from <math id="A2.I27.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, then conditionally on these segments, the laws of the remaining pieces in the slit domain are given by the <em id="A2.I27.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> family with updated neighborhoods. In particular, the growth order of tips can be permuted (“commute”) and yields the same joint law on initial pieces.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I27.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I27.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I27.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I27.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Absolute continuity via a partition function.</em> In the canonical normalization <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, with boundary seeds <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;x_{n}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;x_{n}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a positive smooth function (partition function)</p>
<table id="A2.Ex274" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex274.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=\psi(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=\psi(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I27.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">such that the Loewner driving processes for the local growth of tip <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</p>
<table id="A2.Ex275" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex275.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="dX_{j}(t)=\sqrt{\kappa}\,dB_{j}(t)+\kappa\,\partial_{j}\!\log\psi\big(X(t)\big)\,dt,\qquad\dot{X}_{k}(t)=\frac{2}{X_{k}(t)-X_{j}(t)}\quad(k\neq j)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>κ</mi><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dX_{j}(t)=\sqrt{\kappa}\,dB_{j}(t)+\kappa\,\partial_{j}\!\log\psi\big(X(t)\big)\,dt,\qquad\dot{X}_{k}(t)=\frac{2}{X_{k}(t)-X_{j}(t)}\quad(k\neq j),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I27.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent standard Brownian motions and the other boundary points evolve by the usual Loewner transport.
The function <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> solves the BPZ/null-vector system, for each <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex276" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex276.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\kappa}{2}\,\partial_{jj}\psi\;+\;\sum_{k\neq j}\frac{2}{x_{k}-x_{j}}\,\partial_{k}\psi\;+\;h\sum_{k\neq j}\frac{\psi}{(x_{k}-x_{j})^{2}}\;=\;0,\qquad h=\frac{6-\kappa}{2\kappa}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.335em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mi>ψ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\kappa}{2}\,\partial_{jj}\psi\;+\;\sum_{k\neq j}\frac{2}{x_{k}-x_{j}}\,\partial_{k}\psi\;+\;h\sum_{k\neq j}\frac{\psi}{(x_{k}-x_{j})^{2}}\;=\;0,\qquad h=\frac{6-\kappa}{2\kappa},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I27.i3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">along with the natural scaling covariance. Multiplying <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> by a positive function depending only on <math id="A2.I27.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> leaves the local system unchanged (a gauge freedom).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3) Why “local”?<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
The system specifies the joint law <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p7.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only for the initial segments inside the small neighborhoods</em> <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (and only up to their exit times). It does not define a global law for full curves to their target endpoints. Moreover, the commutation property is <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p7.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local in time</em>: one may grow a short piece of any chosen tip, update the domain, and iterate.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4) Compatible family of measures.<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
If <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are two neighborhood tuples with <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}\subset U^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}\subset U^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, then <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compatibility</em> means:</span></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex277" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex277.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}\quad=\quad\text{the push-forward of }\ \mathbf{P}^{(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}\text{ by restriction to first exit of }U_{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>the push-forward of </mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> by restriction to first exit of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}\quad=\quad\text{the push-forward of }\ \mathbf{P}^{(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}\text{ by restriction to first exit of }U_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">i.e. if <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the exit time of <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the law of
<math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\eta^{(1)}[0,\tau_{1}],\dots,\eta^{(n)}[0,\tau_{n}]\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\eta^{(1)}[0,\tau_{1}],\dots,\eta^{(n)}[0,\tau_{n}]\big)</annotation></semantics></math>
is exactly <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This restriction consistency holds for any nested choice of neighborhoods and is preserved by conformal maps.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remarks.<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<ul id="A2.I28" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I28.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I28.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I28.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A2.I28.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the local construction is known to match a global multiple-SLE measure on <math id="A2.I28.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint simple curves, with partition functions selecting the connectivity pattern.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I28.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I28.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I28.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the cited paper, specific choices of <math id="A2.I28.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> are related to eigenfunctions of (quantum) Calogero–Moser-type operators; the BPZ system above is the analytic avatar of that structure.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard Solution</span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">See the paper <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.16093" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_italic">Multiple chordal SLE(<math class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>) and quantum Calogero–Moser system</a>.
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Set-up.</span> Let <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be simply connected; let <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},\dots,z_{n}\in\partial\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},\dots,z_{n}\in\partial\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct boundary prime ends (“seed” points), and let <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\partial\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\partial\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> be an additional marked boundary point (used for normalization, e.g. <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>).
For each <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, fix a closed neighborhood <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}\subset\overline{\Omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}\subset\overline{\Omega}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}\cap U_{j}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}\cap U_{j}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\notin U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\notin U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1) Concept &amp; context.<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
A <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local multiple chordal SLE<math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> system</em> assigns, to every choice of disjoint neighborhoods <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1},\dots,U_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1},\dots,U_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as above, a law on the <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">initial curve pieces</em> that start at <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and are grown only until they exit <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This assignment is required to be conformally covariant, to satisfy a local domain Markov/commutation property, and to be absolutely continuous with respect to independent chordal SLEs via a smooth positive <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">partition function</em>.
It provides a way to specify the joint law of several interfaces <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">near their seeds</em> without a global specification of full curves; it is complementary to the “global” construction (a single measure on full <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of disjoint curves).</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2) Formal assumptions (axioms).<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
A <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local multiple chordal SLE<math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math></em> on <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u)</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of probability measures</span></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex278" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex278.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">on <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of simple curves <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta^{(1)},\dots,\eta^{(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta^{(1)},\dots,\eta^{(n)})</annotation></semantics></math> such that each <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> starts at <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, is contained in <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> up to its exit time from <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I29" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I29.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I29.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I29.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I29.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Conformal covariance.</em> For any conformal map <math id="A2.I29.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\Omega\to\Omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\Omega\to\Omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which respects the chosen normalization (e.g. <math id="A2.I29.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(u)=u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(u)=u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>), the push-forward of <math id="A2.I29.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="A2.Ex279" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex279.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(\phi(U_{1}),\dots,\phi(U_{n}))}_{\Omega^{\prime};\phi(z_{1}),\dots,\phi(z_{n});\phi(u)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(\phi(U_{1}),\dots,\phi(U_{n}))}_{\Omega^{\prime};\phi(z_{1}),\dots,\phi(z_{n});\phi(u)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I29.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I29.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I29.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I29.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Local domain Markov / commutation.</em> If one reveals initial segments of the curves up to stopping times <math id="A2.I29.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> that are bounded by the exit times from <math id="A2.I29.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, then conditionally on these segments, the laws of the remaining pieces in the slit domain are given by the <em id="A2.I29.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">same</em> family with updated neighborhoods. In particular, the growth order of tips can be permuted (“commute”) and yields the same joint law on initial pieces.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I29.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I29.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I29.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I29.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Absolute continuity via a partition function.</em> In the canonical normalization <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, with boundary seeds <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;x_{n}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;x_{n}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a positive smooth function (partition function)</p>
<table id="A2.Ex280" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex280.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=\psi(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=\psi(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I29.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">such that the Loewner driving processes for the local growth of tip <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are given by</p>
<table id="A2.Ex281" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex281.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="dX_{j}(t)=\sqrt{\kappa}\,dB_{j}(t)+\kappa\,\partial_{j}\!\log\psi\big(X(t)\big)\,dt,\qquad\dot{X}_{k}(t)=\frac{2}{X_{k}(t)-X_{j}(t)}\quad(k\neq j)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>κ</mi><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dX_{j}(t)=\sqrt{\kappa}\,dB_{j}(t)+\kappa\,\partial_{j}\!\log\psi\big(X(t)\big)\,dt,\qquad\dot{X}_{k}(t)=\frac{2}{X_{k}(t)-X_{j}(t)}\quad(k\neq j),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I29.i3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent standard Brownian motions and the other boundary points evolve by the usual Loewner transport.
The function <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> solves the BPZ/null-vector system, for each <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex282" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex282.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\kappa}{2}\,\partial_{jj}\psi\;+\;\sum_{k\neq j}\frac{2}{x_{k}-x_{j}}\,\partial_{k}\psi\;+\;h\sum_{k\neq j}\frac{\psi}{(x_{k}-x_{j})^{2}}\;=\;0,\qquad h=\frac{6-\kappa}{2\kappa}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.335em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mi>ψ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mn> 0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\kappa}{2}\,\partial_{jj}\psi\;+\;\sum_{k\neq j}\frac{2}{x_{k}-x_{j}}\,\partial_{k}\psi\;+\;h\sum_{k\neq j}\frac{\psi}{(x_{k}-x_{j})^{2}}\;=\;0,\qquad h=\frac{6-\kappa}{2\kappa},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I29.i3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">along with the natural scaling covariance. Multiplying <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> by a positive function depending only on <math id="A2.I29.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> leaves the local system unchanged (a gauge freedom).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3) Why “local”?<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p14.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
The system specifies the joint law <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p14.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only for the initial segments inside the small neighborhoods</em> <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (and only up to their exit times). It does not define a global law for full curves to their target endpoints. Moreover, the commutation property is <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p14.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">local in time</em>: one may grow a short piece of any chosen tip, update the domain, and iterate.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4) Compatible family of measures.<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
If <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are two neighborhood tuples with <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}\subset U^{\prime}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}\subset U^{\prime}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, then <em id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compatibility</em> means:</span></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex283" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex283.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}\quad=\quad\text{the push-forward of }\ \mathbf{P}^{(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}\text{ by restriction to first exit of }U_{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>the push-forward of </mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> by restriction to first exit of </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}\quad=\quad\text{the push-forward of }\ \mathbf{P}^{(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})}_{\Omega;z_{1},\dots,z_{n};u}\text{ by restriction to first exit of }U_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">i.e. if <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the exit time of <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U^{\prime}_{1},\dots,U^{\prime}_{n})}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the law of
<math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\eta^{(1)}[0,\tau_{1}],\dots,\eta^{(n)}[0,\tau_{n}]\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\eta^{(1)}[0,\tau_{1}],\dots,\eta^{(n)}[0,\tau_{n}]\big)</annotation></semantics></math>
is exactly <math id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}^{(U_{1},\dots,U_{n})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This restriction consistency holds for any nested choice of neighborhoods and is preserved by conformal maps.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remarks.<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p16.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<ul id="A2.I30" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I30.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I30.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I30.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A2.I30.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the local construction is known to match a global multiple-SLE measure on <math id="A2.I30.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint simple curves, with partition functions selecting the connectivity pattern.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I30.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I30.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I30.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the cited paper, specific choices of <math id="A2.I30.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> are related to eigenfunctions of (quantum) Calogero–Moser-type operators; the BPZ system above is the analytic avatar of that structure.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p17.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="174.94" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 174.94" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,174.94) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#4D4D4D;" fill="#4D4D4D" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 170.31 C 0 172.87 2.07 174.94 4.63 174.94 L 425.01 174.94 C 427.56 174.94 429.64 172.87 429.64 170.31 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 156.07 L 428.94 156.07 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 160.7)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p17.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:24.44em;">
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p17.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p17.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Rubric</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.06em;--ltx-fo-height:9.37em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="131.76" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 129.61)" width="457.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p17.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.06em;">
<span id="A2.I31" class="ltx_enumerate">
<span id="A2.I31.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<span id="A2.I31.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I31.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I31.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Identify the concept and explain its context.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I31.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<span id="A2.I31.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I31.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I31.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Restate the assumptions formally.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I31.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<span id="A2.I31.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I31.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I31.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Explain why it is called a local system.</span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="A2.I31.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<span id="A2.I31.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A2.I31.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I31.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Define the compatible family of measures.</span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
<span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p17.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p17.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Each item is worth 1 point.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS9.SSS1.Px2.p18.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="54.13" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 54.13" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,54.13) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#5A996B;" fill="#5A996B" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 49.51 C 0 52.06 2.07 54.13 4.63 54.13 L 425.01 54.13 C 427.56 54.13 429.64 52.06 429.64 49.51 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F6FFF9;" fill="#F6FFF9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 35.38 L 428.94 35.38 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 40.01)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
