<span id="A2.SS11.SSS2.Px3.p48.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS11.SSS2.Px3.p48.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The explanation is a bit clunky but the prediction is correct.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">12 </span>Creativity</h3>

<section id="A2.SS12.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">(a) </span>Posing new problems and defining new concepts</h4>

<section id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00E000;">Problem 12a1 </h5>

<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span>
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:-10.0pt;"></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="114.15" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 114.15" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,114.15) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#3D6666;" fill="#3D6666" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 109.52 C 0 112.08 2.07 114.15 4.63 114.15 L 425.01 114.15 C 427.56 114.15 429.64 112.08 429.64 109.52 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 95.29 L 428.94 95.29 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 99.92)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:24.44em;">
<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Problem</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 14.66)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.01em;--ltx-fo-height:4.97em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="70.97" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 68.82)" width="442.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.01em;">
<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Define massive SLE and describe the new questions and properties it may raise.</span></span><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" color="#008000" height="18.24" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-4.1px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 18.24 18.24" width="18.24"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#009900;--ltx-fill-color:#009900;--ltx-fg-color:#009900;" color="#009900" fill="#009900" stroke="#009900" stroke-width="1.8pt" transform="translate(0,18.24) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.25,0) translate(0,1.25)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 5.51 0 L 15.75 15.75"></path></g></svg></span>
<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#008000;">GPT-5 thinking passed</span></span>
<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Proposed by Jiaxin Zhang</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Response of GPT-5-thinking</span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition (one–curve, chordal, “massive” deformation).<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Fix a simply connected domain <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with boundary points <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> and a (possibly spatially varying) mass profile <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:D\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:D\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <em id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">massive SLE<sub id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub></em> from <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is the Loewner chain driven by</span></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex357" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex357.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}g_{t}(z)=\frac{2}{g_{t}(z)-W_{t}},\qquad dW_{t}=\sqrt{\kappa}\,dB_{t}+\kappa\,\partial_{x}\!\log Z_{m}\big(g_{t}(\mathbf{z}),W_{t}\big)\,dt," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}g_{t}(z)=\frac{2}{g_{t}(z)-W_{t}},\qquad dW_{t}=\sqrt{\kappa}\,dB_{t}+\kappa\,\partial_{x}\!\log Z_{m}\big(g_{t}(\mathbf{z}),W_{t}\big)\,dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive “massive partition function” (massive martingale) built from <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and the evolving domain <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{t}=D\setminus K_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{t}=D\setminus K_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. via massive Green/Poisson objects), and <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> collects any fixed marked interior/boundary points used to define <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Equivalently, massive SLE<sub id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> is obtained from ordinary SLE<sub id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.2.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> by a Girsanov tilt with the local martingale <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{t}:=Z_{m}(g_{t}(\mathbf{z}),W_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{t}:=Z_{m}(g_{t}(\mathbf{z}),W_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Canonical examples.</em><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<ul id="A2.I46" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I46.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A2.I46.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="A2.I46.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mSLE<sub id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">2</sub> (off–critical LERW).</span> Let <math id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{m}^{D}(z;x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{m}^{D}(z;x)</annotation></semantics></math> be the <em id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">massive Poisson kernel</em>, i.e. the boundary density of the solution <math id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta-m^{2})u=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta-m^{2})u=0</annotation></semantics></math> with boundary mass at <math id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\partial D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\partial D</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a fixed interior <math id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, the observable
<math id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{t}\propto g_{t}^{\prime}(z)^{1/2}\,P_{m}^{D_{t}}(g_{t}(z);W_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{t}\propto g_{t}^{\prime}(z)^{1/2}\,P_{m}^{D_{t}}(g_{t}(z);W_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a local martingale for SLE<sub id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sub">2</sub>, and the corresponding tilt yields
<math id="A2.I46.ix1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dW_{t}=\sqrt{2}\,dB_{t}+2\,\partial_{x}\log P_{m}^{D_{t}}(g_{t}(z);W_{t})\,dt." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dW_{t}=\sqrt{2}\,dB_{t}+2\,\partial_{x}\log P_{m}^{D_{t}}(g_{t}(z);W_{t})\,dt.</annotation></semantics></math>
This is the scaling limit of LERW with bulk killing rate (finite correlation length).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I46.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A2.I46.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math></span> 
<div id="A2.I46.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I46.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I46.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mSLE<sub id="A2.I46.ix2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">4</sub> (massive GFF level lines).</span> Replacing the GFF by the <em id="A2.I46.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">massive</em> GFF (covariance <math id="A2.I46.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-\Delta+m^{2})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-\Delta+m^{2})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>) and tracing suitable level/flow lines gives a drift expressed through <em id="A2.I46.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">massive harmonic</em> boundary data, and thus a massive deformation of SLE<sub id="A2.I46.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sub">4</sub>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conformal covariance.<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Mass destroys exact conformal invariance but preserves a natural covariance:
if <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:D\to D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:D\to D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is conformal, then</span></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex358" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex358.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{m}^{D}(x,\mathbf{z})=\prod_{p\in\{x\}\cup\mathbf{z}}\!|\phi^{\prime}(p)|^{\Delta_{p}}\;Z_{m\star\phi}^{D^{\prime}}\big(\phi(x),\phi(\mathbf{z})\big),\qquad m\star\phi:=|\phi^{\prime}|^{-1}\,m\circ\phi^{-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{m}^{D}(x,\mathbf{z})=\prod_{p\in\{x\}\cup\mathbf{z}}\!|\phi^{\prime}(p)|^{\Delta_{p}}\;Z_{m\star\phi}^{D^{\prime}}\big(\phi(x),\phi(\mathbf{z})\big),\qquad m\star\phi:=|\phi^{\prime}|^{-1}\,m\circ\phi^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">i.e. the mass rescales like an inverse length.
Accordingly, massive SLEs in conformally related domains correspond after a deterministic time change and the mass push–forward <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\star\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\star\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What new properties and questions arise?<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<ul id="A2.I47" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I47.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Crossover &amp; correlation length.</em> With <math id="A2.I47.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there is a scale <math id="A2.I47.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\sim m^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\sim m^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At scales <math id="A2.I47.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ll\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≪</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ll\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, the curve is locally like critical SLE<sub id="A2.I47.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.I47.i1.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> (e.g. Hausdorff dimension <math id="A2.I47.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\kappa/8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\kappa/8</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.I47.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>), while macroscopic events (crossings, arm exponents) exhibit <em id="A2.I47.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exponential</em> decay on scale <math id="A2.I47.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Quantify these crossovers rigorously.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I47.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Restriction/locality are broken.</em> Classical restriction (exact for <math id="A2.I47.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=\tfrac{8}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=\tfrac{8}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>) and locality (<math id="A2.I47.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=6</annotation></semantics></math>) fail once <math id="A2.I47.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
What are the sharp “massive” replacements (e.g. quasi–restriction inequalities, defect martingales)?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I47.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reversibility.</em> Ordinary SLE<sub id="A2.I47.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.I47.i3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> is reversible for <math id="A2.I47.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.
Does massive SLE<sub id="A2.I47.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.I47.i3.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> remain reversible (after the natural change of <math id="A2.I47.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> when swapping endpoints)?
Identify the precise Radon–Nikodym derivative between forward/backward laws.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I47.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Absolute continuity windows.</em> For fixed <math id="A2.I47.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, massive SLE is absolutely continuous w.r.t. SLE up to hitting times of marked sets.
Obtain quantitative density bounds in terms of massive Green/Poisson functionals.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I47.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Multiple curves &amp; massive BPZ.</em> For <math id="A2.I47.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> curves, the commutation framework persists with a <em id="A2.I47.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">massive partition function</em> <math id="A2.I47.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
What PDEs replace the BPZ/null–vector equations?
Heuristically: modify the level–two equations by replacing harmonic objects with massive ones (e.g. <math id="A2.I47.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta-m^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta-m^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in Ward identities), and track how <math id="A2.I47.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> enters commutators.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I47.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Near–critical scaling limits.</em> Interfaces of near–critical lattice models (FK–Ising, percolation with field, dimer with monomer density, etc.) are expected to converge to massive SLE<sub id="A2.I47.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.I47.i6.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> with model–dependent <math id="A2.I47.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.
Prove tightness/identification in specific models, beyond the LERW/killing case.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I47.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Couplings with massive fields.</em> Extend SLE/GFF couplings to the massive GFF (for <math id="A2.I47.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=4</annotation></semantics></math>) and to massive fermionic/parafermionic fields (for <math id="A2.I47.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=2,3</annotation></semantics></math>), and characterize the drift via massive harmonic extensions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I47.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Loop–soup/cluster pictures.</em> Relate massive SLE to <em id="A2.I47.i8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">massive Brownian loop soups</em> and near–critical CLE; identify intensity/mass relations and percolative thresholds.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I47.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I47.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I47.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I47.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Exact formulas?</em> In <math id="A2.I47.i9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, massive Poisson/Green kernels are explicit (modified Bessel functions).
Can one obtain closed drifts and solvable cases (e.g. hitting distributions, left–passage probabilities) for mSLE<sub id="A2.I47.i9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.I47.i9.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub>?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Summary.<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Massive SLE<sub id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> is an off–critical, scale–dependent deformation of SLE obtained by tilting SLE with martingales built from <em id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">massive</em> (i.e. <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta-m^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta-m^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>) objects.
It preserves the Loewner/Markov mechanism and conformal <em id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">covariance</em> (with <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> rescaling like an inverse length), but breaks exact conformal invariance and classical restriction/locality, leading to new crossover phenomena and PDE structures.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard Solution</span>.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Massive SLE<sub id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub>.<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Given a simply connected domain <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with boundary points <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> and a mass profile <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:D\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:D\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>, a <em id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">massive SLE<sub id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub></em> is defined as the Loewner chain driven by</span></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex359" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex359.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}g_{t}(z)=\frac{2}{g_{t}(z)-W_{t}},\qquad dW_{t}=\sqrt{\kappa}\,dB_{t}+\kappa\,\partial_{x}\log Z_{m}(g_{t}(\mathbf{z}),W_{t})\,dt," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}g_{t}(z)=\frac{2}{g_{t}(z)-W_{t}},\qquad dW_{t}=\sqrt{\kappa}\,dB_{t}+\kappa\,\partial_{x}\log Z_{m}(g_{t}(\mathbf{z}),W_{t})\,dt,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive massive partition function built from <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and the evolving domain. Equivalently, it arises from SLE<sub id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> via a Girsanov tilt with martingale <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{t}=Z_{m}(g_{t}(\mathbf{z}),W_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{t}=Z_{m}(g_{t}(\mathbf{z}),W_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Examples.<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p10.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<ul id="A2.I48" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I48.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I48.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I48.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I48.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mSLE<sub id="A2.I48.i1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">2</sub></em>: scaling limit of loop-erased random walk with bulk killing, with drift determined by the massive Poisson kernel.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I48.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I48.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I48.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I48.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mSLE<sub id="A2.I48.i2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">4</sub></em>: level/flow lines of the massive Gaussian free field, with drift given by massive harmonic boundary data.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conformal covariance.<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Mass breaks exact conformal invariance but preserves covariance: if <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:D\to D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:D\to D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex360" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex360.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{m}^{D}(x,\mathbf{z})=\prod_{p}|\phi^{\prime}(p)|^{\Delta_{p}}\,Z_{m\star\phi}^{D^{\prime}}(\phi(x),\phi(\mathbf{z})),\quad m\star\phi:=|\phi^{\prime}|^{-1}m\circ\phi^{-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>D</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mi>p</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋆</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{m}^{D}(x,\mathbf{z})=\prod_{p}|\phi^{\prime}(p)|^{\Delta_{p}}\,Z_{m\star\phi}^{D^{\prime}}(\phi(x),\phi(\mathbf{z})),\quad m\star\phi:=|\phi^{\prime}|^{-1}m\circ\phi^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">New properties and open questions.<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p12.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
<ul id="A2.I49" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I49.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I49.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I49.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I49.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Restriction/locality:</em> classical properties fail; seek massive analogues.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I49.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I49.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I49.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I49.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reversibility:</em> open for <math id="A2.I49.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> under massive deformation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I49.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I49.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I49.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I49.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Near-critical scaling:</em> interfaces of near-critical models (FK–Ising, percolation, dimers) conjecturally converge to massive SLE.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Summary.<span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p13.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Massive SLE<sub id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p13.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p13.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">κ</span></sub> is an off-critical deformation of SLE preserving the Loewner–Markov structure and conformal covariance (with <math id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> scaling as inverse length). It introduces scale dependence, breaks exact conformal invariance, and raises new analytic and probabilistic challenges.
∎</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.SS12.SSS1.Px1.p14.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="69.62" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 429.64 69.62" width="429.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,69.62) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#4D4D4D;" fill="#4D4D4D" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 65 C 0 67.55 2.07 69.62 4.63 69.62 L 425.01 69.62 C 427.56 69.62 429.64 67.55 429.64 65 L 429.64 4.63 C 429.64 2.07 427.56 0 425.01 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F9F9F9;" fill="#F9F9F9" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 50.76 L 428.94 50.76 L 428.94 4.63 C 428.94 2.45 427.18 0.69 425.01 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 55.39)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:24.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="338.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
